// Snow exists to be temporary. It is the only material in the set whose
// melting point sits *below* ambient (20 degrees), which is not a mistake:
// snow does not survive in a temperate world on its own, it survives while
// the cold front that brought it is overhead. Weather lays a band of cold
// over the ground it is falling on; when the front passes, that cold lifts,
// every flake is instantly above its melting point, and the drift turns to
// meltwater over the following seconds.
//
// That gets thaw for free from the existing upward phase change and needs no
// freezing code at all -- water does not become snow, weather simply makes
// more of it while it is cold.
(
    name: "snow",
    display: "Snow",
    kind: Powder,
    // Trivially diggable. Snow should never be an obstacle you have to
    // work at; walking through a drift is the intended experience.
    penetration_resistance: 0.2,
    // Light -- fresh snow is roughly a tenth the density of the rock it
    // lands on, and this is what makes a drift sit *on* terrain rather
    // than behaving like another kind of sand.
    density: 0.3,
    // A high angle of repose, so drifts hold a steep face and pile into
    // banks against anything upright instead of spreading out flat. This
    // is the single number that decides whether snow reads as snow.
    friction_angle: 42.0,
    // See Material::blast_resistance -- trivially blown through, same
    // spirit as the trivial penetration_resistance above.
    blast_resistance: 0.2,
    melting_point: 2.0,
    // **Not optional, and its absence was a real bug**, not a tuning
    // choice: `fire.rs`'s `diffuse_heat` early-exits at conductivity <= 0,
    // so a flake the storm had chilled below 2 degrees could never warm
    // back up. It sat at storm temperature forever, permanently
    // off-ambient, keeping its own chunk awake for the rest of the run --
    // and the wiki's promise that "drifts thaw when the front passes" was
    // only ever true of the cells a storm never actually reached. The
    // probe for it is `fire.rs`'s
    // `a_snow_drift_chilled_by_a_storm_thaws_after_it_passes`, which was
    // written `#[ignore]`d against this exact gap: at conductivity 0 it
    // panicked at its 2000-frame budget; with this line the flake warms
    // -6 -> -4 -> -2 -> 0 -> 2 and melts at frame 3.
    //
    // 0.08 matches water's, which is a coincidence worth stating rather
    // than a derivation: real snow insulates far better than water. What
    // this number sets in practice is how long a drift takes to give up
    // the storm's cold once the storm has gone, and matching the meltwater
    // it turns into means a thawing drift and the puddle under it settle
    // back to ambient together instead of one of them holding the chunk
    // awake after the other is done.
    heat_conductivity: 0.08,
    melts_into: "water",
    colors: [
        (246, 248, 253),
        (232, 237, 246),
        (252, 253, 255),
        (220, 228, 240),
    ],
)
